# Code Style and Requirements Guideline

This document outlines the code style and requirements for SquirrelJME. All
code and contributions must follow these.

# Universal

These apply everywhere, including both C and Java, in all files and
documentation.

## All Inputs/Parameters Must Be Checked, No Assumptions

All inputs and parameters must be checked for validity so that any invalid
parameters for a method or otherwise must fail. This holds true for all
methods, even if it is known at the current time that the input value will
always be valid. By checking and failing with specific inputs, any and all
assumptions are removed.

Such input validation may seem pedantic and verbose, however it is very
possible for the contract to suddenly change or that a method will be moved,
repurposed, forgotten about, or otherwise. Such current method must have
its own contract written in place. This improved the stability of the project
and increases accessibility during debugging as it may also enable the callee
to debug the caller.

## Fail Fast

Everything is fail fast, this means that when an error condition or unknown
state is reached that the program fails at the soonest possible moment. It
must be belay exceptions nor ignore exceptions unless required to do so.

Failing fast allows for more accessible debugging.

## Incomplete Code Must NEVER No-Op

It must always be assumed that incomplete code will never be completed ever
in the future, or if it will be that it may be many years in the future. As
such, all 

Methods which do nothing is a contract that it does nothing, and as such that
has a side effect of doing nothing and whatever that entails. Failing indicates
that the program is no longer valid.

Incomplete code is a failure condition and state.

